{"name":"Polka Dot Plum","hex":"#5a4458","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"polka","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lively dance originating in Bohemia.","The music for this dance."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dance the polka."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/polka"},{"term":"dot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Dass."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, round spot.","A punctuation mark used to indicate the end of a sentence or an abbreviated part of a word; a full stop; a period."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dot"},{"term":"plum","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fruit and its tree.\n An edible, fleshy stone fruit of species Prunus domestica (European plum) and Prunus americana American plum, often of a dark red or purple colour. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\nA stone-fruit tree which bears this fruit, Prunus domestica. \nThe edible, fleshy stone fruit of several species resembling the plum, or the tree from which they grow. \nPrunus sect. Prunus\nPrunus cerasifera (cherry plum or myrobalan).\nPrunus salicina (Chinese plum or Japanese plum).\nPrunus spinosa (sloe).\nPrunus ursina (bear's plum).\nPrunus sect. Prunocerasus (North American plums)\nPrunus americana (American plum).\nPrunus angustifolia (Chickasaw plum or sand plum).\nPrunus hortulana (hortulan plum).\nPrunus nigra (Canadian plum or black plum).\nPrunus rivularis (creek plum or hog plum).\nPrunus subcordata (Klamath plum or Oregon plum).\nPrunus sect. Armeniaca (better known as apricots)\nPrunus mume, an Asian fruit more closely related to the apricot than the plum, usually consumed pickled, dried, or as a juice or wine; ume.\n A dried grape or raisin, as used in a pudding or cake.","An edible, fleshy stone fruit of species Prunus domestica (European plum) and Prunus americana American plum, often of a dark red or purple colour. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark bluish-red colour.","Choice; especially lavish or preferred."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plum"}],"entries":[{"title":"Polka dot","blocks":[{"text":"The polka dot, also written polkadot, and also called spot printed and spot print in the United Kingdom and pois (\"peas\") in France, is a pattern consisting of an array of large filled circles of the same size. Though the dots must be relatively large ones, and must be all the same size in any given print, different styles of polka dot prints may change the size of the dots or the distance between them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Polka_dots.svg/330px-Polka_dots.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polka_dot","wikidata":"Q2429962"},{"title":"Polka","blocks":[{"text":"Polka is a dance style and genre of dance music in 24 originating in nineteenth-century Bohemia, now part of the Czech Republic. Though generally associated with Czech and Central European culture, polka is popular throughout Europe and the Americas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/La_Polka.jpg/330px-La_Polka.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polka","wikidata":"Q153071"},{"title":"Plum","blocks":[{"text":"A plum is a fruit of some species in Prunus subg. Prunus. Dried plums are usually called prunes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Plums_African_Rose_-_whole%2C_halved_and_slice.jpg/330px-Plums_African_Rose_-_whole%2C_halved_and_slice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plum","wikidata":"Q12372598"},{"title":"Plum (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Plum is a purple color with a brownish-gray tinge, like that shown on the right, or a reddish purple, which is a close representation of the average color of the plum fruit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Plum_on_tree02.jpg/330px-Plum_on_tree02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plum_(color)","wikidata":"Q3108722"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/2021_SLT_Weert_podium_Alison_Jackson_polkadot_jersey.jpg/500px-2021_SLT_Weert_podium_Alison_Jackson_polkadot_jersey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/2021_SLT_Weert_podium_Alison_Jackson_polkadot_jersey.jpg/500px-2021_SLT_Weert_podium_Alison_Jackson_polkadot_jersey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alison Jackson wears a Queen of the Mountains polkadot cycling jersey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2021_SLT_Weert_podium_Alison_Jackson_polkadot_jersey.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric_Bazille_001.jpg/120px-Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric_Bazille_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric_Bazille_001.jpg/250px-Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric_Bazille_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric_Bazille_001.jpg/120px-Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric_Bazille_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric_Bazille_001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Publicity_handout_of_Norma_Smallwood%2C_of_Tulsa%2C_Oklahoma%2C_winner_of_Miss_America_in_1926%2C_posing_in_a_Polka_Dots_bathing_suit.png/120